The producer's **three stated defeats are genuinely closed** and every "before/after" exit code in their §1–§3 reproduces exactly (I rebuilt each case myself): DEFEAT A/B/C were `rc=0` on the original script and are `rc=1` on the hardened one, for the intended reasons, and the hardened guard still PASSes the current tree and still emits exactly **111** pre-fix FAIL lines (41/33/37). The header's four invariants do match the code as the header words them. **But the hardening closes the three defeats only in their shortest form, and I found six defeat classes that still produce `rc=0` on a real divergence** — the headline ones being (1) the "reason" test is shape-only, so the *minimal* bypass is four nonsense words totalling 24 characters, (2) Haxe honours leading-whitespace / trailing-whitespace / trailing-CR root lines that the guard's regex silently drops, (3) `--macro include('pkg')` / `haxe.macro.Compiler.include('pkg')` injects modules into one side of a pair and the guard never reads `--macro` lines at all, (4) `-cp` divergence is not checked (drop `-cp samples` from `swift-f32.hxml` → PASS), (5) a root deleted from **both** files is only caught when the module declares `@:test`, and (6) a real `@:test` module dropped from both files can be silenced by one junk-shaped `unrootedTestModules` line. Items 1–3 are *new executable defeats* with `rc=0` measured on the hardened script.

